      "explain": "A. The scene is one moment in Israel's story under a king who has led the nation to Baal, and at the top level it is God showing who is God. Elijah's confrontation is reported, not commanded, and nothing in the text turns it into a technique for anyone else. Note that this sermon is not stupid, which is why it is the example: it has read the chapter, it has taken the covenant crisis seriously, and it has still turned a report into an instruction. B mislabels the genre by the categories this lesson uses. 1 Kings is a story about a prophet rather than a collection of oracles, which is what lesson 4 will treat as prophecy. Worth knowing that the Jewish arrangement puts Joshua, Judges, Samuel and Kings together as the Former Prophets, so a Jewish reader would call Kings prophecy in a different sense of the word, and that is a difference about how the shelf is labelled rather than about how this chapter works. C treats the fire as an endorsement of everything Elijah did on the way to it, which is silence read as approval. D confuses the levels and invents a rule the lesson never gives."
