      "answer": 3,
      "explain": "D. The genre question comes before the truth question, and this chapter puts the seam where you can see it. The prose account stops to quote two lines of poetry and names where it got them, and the same collection is quoted again for David's lament at 2 Samuel 1:18. Deciding what a victory poem claims, and how that relates to the prose around it, is the first move, and Christians who hold the passage true and inerrant read those lines in different ways. This course does not settle that and does not need to. The point is the order of the questions. A runs the error in the opposite direction from the usual one, letting what we find plausible decide what the text is allowed to say, and it is still putting the truth question first. B offers a menu the lesson refuses; history or legend is not a genre choice, and neither word describes Hebrew narrative well. C is the honest beginner's answer, and it is why this lesson exists. A narrative book can quote a poem, a song, a letter, a law and a list, and Joshua 10 quotes a poem out loud and tells you where it got it."
