      "answer": 3,
      "explain": "Compression is standard in ancient writing: an author reports that a man did what he did through agents as though he did it himself, and no first-century reader thought a claim about seating arrangements was being made. Option A has the direction backwards, and it's worth seeing why that matters: Luke's version has more detail and more people, and a writer adding an embassy is doing something quite different from a writer shortening one. Option B is possible in principle and nothing forbids it, but two centurions with the same servant, the same speech about authority and the same commendation in the same town is a great deal to hold to avoid a difficulty that the genre already explains. Option C invents a reliability ranking the dating evidence doesn't support. Note that \"compression\" is a category modern evangelicals argue about; the Chicago Statement's own list covers selection and arrangement, and whether it stretches this far is disputed among people who all hold to inerrancy. Michael Licona's use of it drew a public objection from Norman Geisler in 2011, so this is a live quarrel inside that camp rather than a settled allowance."
