  "summary": "Write so that people understand you the first time. Built on what research says readers actually do with a sentence, not on a list of rules you were handed at school.",
  "description": "Clear writing is not a matter of taste and it is not politeness. It is the difference between being understood and not. This course teaches the reader-expectation tradition: where a reader looks for the point, which word they will emphasise, what happens when a subject and its verb drift apart, and why an action buried in a noun is hard work to read. It covers the sentence, the paragraph and the whole document, it is honest about where the famous rules came from and which of them survive contact with evidence, and it ends with the step almost no writing course teaches: finding out from a real reader whether any of it worked.",
