      "answer": 3,
      "explain": "Straight means the rate is the same everywhere on the line, so the level rises by the same amount in every equal stretch of time. A curve would mean the rate changes as you go, either speeding up or slowing down. Option B is the interesting distractor and it is half right in the real world: the shape of the tank does affect how fast the level rises for a given flow, so a tank that widens at the top would give a curve even with a steady hose. But the question asks what the straight line tells you, and the answer is about the rate rather than the vessel. Option C confuses a curve with a downward slope, which is a different thing: a falling line is still straight."
